# Commits that touched many lines without changing what any of them mean.
# `git blame` must skip them, and this file is how it learns to:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Why it matters beyond readability: the `comment` check in agent-refactor uses
# blame to tell "the code changed and the comment did not" from "they agree".
# A repo-wide mechanical edit resets the code side's author-time on every line it
# touched, so every normative comment sitting above one of them reads as
# `conflict` -- which the tool reports as **a bug to hand out**, the most
# expensive false positive it can produce.

